  :doc/body "# Inference channel 01 - what a caller learns about data it cannot read one value of

> root ADR-2609108000 says: measure the channel before adding a defence.
> This is that measurement. It was expected to find the reviewed threat
> (an aggregate-only surface answering 0/1); it found a different one, in a
> function whose docstring correctly calls itself safe.

## S. The threat that was reviewed does not exist in this shape

The external review's example was `count(where(...))` allowed while rows are
denied. That presupposes an aggregate-only mode. This plane has none: the
only visibility seam is `visible?`, it is per-datom, and it is required
rather than defaulted. A caller either sees a datom or does not, and section
C of the harness confirms values really are hidden - `q` returns the empty
set and `entity-attrs` PRUNES the entity rather than returning empty sets
under its keys.

**So the reviewed attack is not applicable, and the applicable one is next
to it.**

## T. materialize takes no visible? in either arity

    materialize : [store coll-keys]  |  [store coll-keys max-datoms]

`visible?` is applied by `q` OVER the materialized plane. The namespace
docstring says this is safe by construction, and for VALUES it is - measured,
section C. It is not a claim about cardinality, and the ceiling that bounds
the scan reports the cardinality it refused at, in `ex-data`, with `visible?`
never consulted:

    {:kotobase.query/error :materialize-over-budget
     :datoms 6 :max-datoms 1 :collection \"clinical\"}

## U. What was recovered, under a policy that hides every clinical datom

Fixture: 3 clinical documents (4, 2 and 5 fields), 2 public documents.
Policy: nothing whose entity namespace is `clinical` is ever visible.
Unit is PROBES, not wall clock - this workstation runs concurrent agents.

| channel | recovered | cost | needs |
|---|---|---|---|
| A. one call | 6 datoms + the collection name | 1 probe | 3-arity |
| B. exact total, clinical | 17 (truth 17) | 12 probes | 3-arity |
| B. exact total, whole store | 25 (truth 25) | 12 probes | 3-arity |
| B2. at the built-in ceiling | 200004 datoms | 1 call | **2-arity** |
| D. existence of one named individual | delta 7 | 24 probes | 3-arity |
| E. that individual's field count | 5 (truth 5) | 24 probes | 3-arity |

**B2 is the one that matters for deployment.** A wire surface that never lets
a caller name `max-datoms` is not therefore silent: the built-in ceiling is a
threshold like any other, and crossing it reports the running total. Two
stores either side of 200000 datoms separate cleanly, 2-arity only.

D is the reviewed threat arriving by another road. Two stores differing only
by whether one named person has a clinical record produce different recovered
totals. No value of that person's was read, and `visible?` refused every one
of her datoms.

## V. Both directions, and the boundary

The harness returns a COUNT of failures, not a boolean, and refuses (exit 2,
neither 0 nor 1) when its own control does not hold.

- Control broken (a policy that hides nothing): exit 2, `REFUSING to report
  the channel`, and the two hidden-value assertions fail while the
  policy-is-not-vacuous assertion still passes. The refusal path is not
  theoretical.
- Comparison flipped, `(> n max-datoms)` to `(>= n max-datoms)`: exit 1 with
  4 failures, and the two that name the boundary are among them. Without a
  probe at exactly `n` the operator is invisible - every other probe passes
  under both spellings.
- Restored: exit 0, 13 assertions.

## W. What this does NOT say

- It does not say the ceiling is a mistake. It bounds work, it aborts rather
  than reports, and its docstring explains why. The finding is that a bound
  on work is not a bound on disclosure, and this one publishes the quantity
  it bounded.
- It does not measure a deployed surface. It measures the library seam. Which
  callers expose the 3-arity over a wire is a separate inventory, not done.
- It proposes no fix. Choosing between a coarsened refusal, a visible?-aware
  count, and accepting the disclosure is a decision, and this is the input to
  it.

